@page "/docs/registry/directory"
<PageTitle>Get listed - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Get Listed</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">pull request</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl text-muted-foreground">The community directory is the reviewed index of public registries and themes. A listed registry appears on the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="community">community page</a>, and the CLI consults the directory when someone references your <code>@("@namespace")</code> without having recorded it: the entry is offered, and on acceptance recorded into their <code>blaizio.json</code>. Your registry's content stays on your host; the listing is a pointer, and adding one is a pull request.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When you do not need it</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A public repository.</b> <code>owner/repo/item</code> addresses install without any listing. The directory exists for <code>@("@namespace")</code>s.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A team or private registry.</b> Consumers record it directly with <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry add" />; the directory only lists public registries.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Trying things out.</b> A local folder path or a direct URL already works as a registry, unlisted.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Requirements</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li>The built output is publicly reachable over HTTPS - <code>&lt;url&gt;/index.json</code> answers without credentials.</li>
            <li>The manifest passes <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry validate" />.</li>
            <li>The entry's <code>name</code> is the <code>@("@namespace")</code> consumers will use, and it is not already taken in the directory.</li>
            <li>The description is one accurate line about what the registry ships.</li>
            <li>The homepage is a real page about the registry or its publisher.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Review checks all of these. Listings are pointers maintained by their authors and are not audited by Blaizio - the CLI's trust gates still apply when anyone installs.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Listing a registry</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Add an entry to <code>docs/Blaizio.Docs/wwwroot/community/registries.json</code>: your namespace, homepage, the built registry URL, and one line about what it ships.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/CommunityRegistryEntry" />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Listing a theme</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Themes go in <code>docs/Blaizio.Docs/wwwroot/community/themes.json</code> and carry their full data in the entry: the same <code>light</code> and <code>dark</code> maps a theme item uses, plus a title and your handle. Nothing to host, and the theme becomes applyable and copyable on the community page directly.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/CommunityThemeEntry" />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The steps</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li>Fork <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/blaizio/blaizio" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blaizio/blaizio</a>, or use GitHub's pencil button on the file, which forks for you.</li>
            <li>Add your entry to the right file, keeping the existing formatting.</li>
            <li>Open the pull request.</li>
            <li>Once merged, the next docs deployment shows your entry on <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="community">the community page</a> and in the directory the CLI consults.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The directory is a fallback, not a silent resolver: when a consumer references your <code>@("@namespace")</code> unrecorded, the CLI shows your entry and asks before recording it - after that, their project resolves it locally like any registry they added themselves.</p>
    </section>
</div>
